points.explanation:
{にかかわらず} says that a result is the same **no matter which option or value** applies. It therefore follows words that contain a range or a pair of opposites:
- nouns like {天候[てんこう]} (weather), {年齢[ねんれい]} (age), {有無[うむ]} (presence or absence), {大小[だいしょう]} (large or small), {好[す]き嫌[きら]い}
- verb pairs: {参加[さんか]するしない}, {行[い]く行[い]かない}

It is formal and common in rules, notices and job ads. Hiragana {かかわらず} is standard, though {関[かか]わらず} also appears.

Don't confuse it with {にもかかわらず} ("despite"), which reacts to **one concrete fact**.

These patterns set up an **assumption** and reason from it: "supposing X, then Y". They're used for:
- pure hypotheticals: {生[う]まれ変[か]われるとしたら} ("if I could be reborn")
- information you've just heard and are taking as true: {その話[はなし]が本当[ほんとう]だとすれば、大変[たいへん]だ}
- calculations: {八時間[はちじかん]働[はたら]くとすると、週[しゅう]に四十時間[よんじゅうじかん]だ}

{仮[かり]に} or {もし} often comes first. {としたら} is the most conversational; {とすれば} and {とすると} sound more logical and are common in discussions and writing.

This is the standard humble pattern for **your own action that affects the other person**: sending, contacting, guiding, explaining. {いたします} is the humble form of {します}, so {お〜いたします} is a step more humble than {お〜します}.

- {お}: mostly with native Japanese verbs: {お送[おく]りいたします}, {お持[も]ちいたします}, {お預[あず]かりいたします}
- {ご}: mostly with Sino-Japanese {する} nouns: {ご連絡[れんらく]いたします}, {ご案内[あんない]いたします}, {ご説明[せつめい]いたします}
- Common exceptions: {お電話[でんわ]いたします}, {お返事[へんじ]いたします}

Don't use it for actions that don't involve the listener (✗ {お食[た]べいたします}), and don't confuse it with respectful {ご〜ください} / {お〜になる}, which describe the **other person's** actions.

{ございます} is the super-polite form of {あります}, and {でございます} is the super-polite form of {です}. They are standard in **customer service, phone calls and formal announcements**:
- answering the phone: {はい、山田商事[やまだしょうじ]でございます}
- giving directions: {お手洗[てあら]いは二階[にかい]にございます}
- in emails: {ご不明[ふめい]な点[てん]がございましたら} ("if you have any questions")

{でございます} is used for yourself, your company and things, **not for the customer**. To ask "Are you Mr Yamada?", use the respectful {山田様[やまださま]でいらっしゃいますか}; ✗ {山田様[やまださま]でございますか}.
